-- ============================================================================
-- DC24 - Rock Physics PROFESSIONAL (intermediate tier) goes deep.
--
-- Flips (rockphysics, intermediate) onto the DC1 deep path by inserting its
-- ACTIVE academy_course_structures row, generated from the repo manifest
-- src/content/courses/rockphysics/intermediate/manifest.json (emitted by a
-- generator, not hand-typed), and seeding the six module quiz banks plus the
-- final exam. Answer keys live only here and in the table.
--
-- Six modules, 27 lessons: putting the fluid in the rock, the dry frame,
-- substituting the fluid, the gas effect, when there is no shear log, and the
-- substitution workflow.
--
-- Every numeric item traces to the rockphysics teaching oracle in
-- src/lib/rockphysicsTeaching.js and reproduces the live NG6 Professional
-- capstone answer key: shear modulus 7.29 GPa, in-situ saturated modulus 13.32
-- GPa, dry frame 7.350343061720982 GPa, gas case vp 2905.6972280296195 m/s, gas
-- case density 2038.7104517793223 kg/m3 and a Greenberg-Castagna shear estimate
-- of 1521.197276567149 m/s at vp 3000. substitutionLab.test.js pins the panel
-- math to those same values.
--
-- Tier positioning: the Associate tier (DC10) owns the ingredients, which are
-- properties of substances rather than of rocks (Batzle-Wang fluids, the
-- Voigt-Reuss-Hill mineral frame, Wood's mixed pore fluid), and it forward-quotes
-- this tier's headline numbers. Nothing here re-teaches them. This tier owns the
-- rock: the first two graded values are exact integers in disguise (2250 x 1800^2
-- = 7.29e9 and 2250 x 3200^2 - 4/3 x 7.29e9 = 13.32e9, both hand-reachable with
-- no rounding, which is why their tolerance is 0.01); inverse Gassmann recovers a
-- dry frame of 7.350343061720982 GPa, which is NOT the mineral frame
-- (30.87940062475596 GPa) and gives a Biot coefficient of 0.80; the substituted
-- density is mass bookkeeping rather than rock physics; the shear velocity RISES
-- 1800 -> 1890.9758806113214 m/s because the modulus is Gassmann-invariant and the
-- density fell; the round trip back to brine is exact, which is the tier's best
-- quality control; the porosity assumption is worth five times the mineral modulus
-- range (324 m/s over 0.20 to 0.30 against 60 m/s over 35 to 40 GPa) and the
-- inverse relation refuses outright below a porosity of about 0.14; the gas effect
-- SATURATES, so vp is not monotonic in saturation (minimum 2830.2791905880454 m/s
-- at Sw 0.73, below the pure gas 2905.6972280296195) and one percent of gas
-- delivers 32.76 pct of the drop; and where a shear log is missing the
-- Greenberg-Castagna composite can be tested on this very well, where it
-- under-predicts the measured 1800 m/s by 120.05415453482057 m/s.
--
-- Bank standard: measured on the length RANK of the correct option and checked
-- for cyclic key sequences, answer-index skew, within-pool near-duplicates and
-- same-claim option pairs before seeding. Across the 132 questions the correct
-- option is longest in 21 pct and shortest in 21 pct.
--
-- Enrollment grandfathering: enforced_from defaults to now() on first insert; the
-- ON CONFLICT update deliberately leaves enforced_from unchanged on re-seeds.
--
-- Ladder note: an INTERMEDIATE enrollment needs an unrevoked ASSOCIATE
-- certification on the SAME app, so the dry-run probes back-stop a rockphysics
-- associate cert in-transaction, and a P3b check proves the rockphysics Associate
-- tier is left undisturbed at 132 questions.
--
-- New rp-substitution-explorer panel. NO DDL. Data only.
-- ============================================================================

values ('rockphysics', 'intermediate', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-putting-the-fluid-in-the-rock","title":"Putting the Fluid in the Rock","lesson_keys":["l01-what-this-tier-adds","l02-moduli-from-a-log-point","l03-the-two-directions","l04-the-substitution-panel-map"]},{"key":"m02-the-dry-frame","title":"The Dry Frame","lesson_keys":["l01-inverse-gassmann","l02-not-the-mineral-frame","l03-shear-is-fluid-blind","l04-what-the-dry-frame-depends-on","l05-when-inverse-gassmann-refuses"]},{"key":"m03-substituting-the-fluid","title":"Substituting the Fluid","lesson_keys":["l01-the-forward-step","l02-density-is-the-easy-half","l03-why-vs-goes-up","l04-impedance-and-vp-over-vs","l05-the-oil-case"]},{"key":"m04-the-gas-effect","title":"The Gas Effect","lesson_keys":["l01-the-first-percent","l02-the-velocity-minimum","l03-why-seismic-cannot-count-gas","l04-what-that-means-for-a-prospect"]},{"key":"m05-when-there-is-no-shear-log","title":"When There Is No Shear Log","lesson_keys":["l01-why-shear-goes-missing","l02-the-mudrock-line","l03-greenberg-castagna","l04-testing-the-estimator","l05-estimate-first-substitute-after"]},{"key":"m06-the-substitution-workflow","title":"The Substitution Workflow","lesson_keys":["l01-the-workflow-end-to-end","l02-quality-control","l03-the-capstone-walkthrough","l04-onward-to-expert"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
